# Environment
NODE_ENV=development

# Server
PORT=3080

# Puppeteer Configuration (optional)
# Custom Chrome/Chromium executable path (useful for CI/CD or custom installations)
# PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium-browser

# Browser Launch Profile (optional, but REQUIRED in production for Cloudflare-gated stores)
# clean-headful = the proven recipe for passing Cloudflare's non-interactive JS challenge with no
# solver and no human: a real, current Chrome launched HEADFUL on the Ozone headless platform (no X
# server / Xvfb needed), with --enable-automation stripped and a minimal flag list. Measured
# 2026-09-07: headless:true FAILS, and a stealth plugin on an older Chrome FAILS. Unset (or any
# other value) keeps the historical headless profile, which is what CI and the tests use -- and a
# set-but-unrecognised value logs ONE boot warning and falls back to it. A store that DECLARES a
# Cloudflare gate (searchFetch.access: 'cloudflare') is REFUSED on the headless profile rather than
# fetched: the challenge cannot pass there, and the attempt still costs the egress IP's reputation.
# BROWSER_LAUNCH_MODE=clean-headful
#
# PROCESS timezone -- REQUIRED in production for Cloudflare-gated stores.
# The challenge runs in a cross-origin frame that reads the BROWSER PROCESS's zone, not anything a
# page emulates. Measured 2026-09-07: TZ=UTC with page.emulateTimezone('America/Chicago') stays
# stuck on the interstitial forever; TZ=America/Chicago with NO emulation clears in ~8s. A container
# with no TZ is UTC, so this fails silently -- nothing errors, gated stores just never clear.
# TZ=America/Chicago
#
# OPTIONAL per-page timezone emulation per EGRESS (CDP page.emulateTimezone), on top of TZ. These do
# NOT clear a challenge -- only TZ does. They exist so a page's own reported zone matches the exit it
# leaves through, for the fingerprinting a store does after the challenge. Left EMPTY by the
# deployment; an unset variable means "emulate nothing" (the page keeps the process zone).
# RESIDENTIAL_EGRESS_TIMEZONE=America/Chicago
# DIRECT_EGRESS_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# Admin Features (optional)
# Token for admin-only endpoints like /reset-pool (non-production only)
# ADMIN_TOKEN=your-admin-token-here

# Plugin Directory (optional)
# Directory scanned for ruleset plugin packages at boot. Defaults to the
# service's own node_modules. Point this at a mounted volume to inject
# plugins at runtime without installing them as dependencies.
# PLUGIN_DIR=/plugins/node_modules

# MFC Cookie Security (required for authenticated scraping)
# Whitelist of cookie names allowed when scraping MFC with authentication
# These cookies are filtered from user-provided session data for security
# Default: PHPSESSID,sesUID,sesDID,cf_clearance
MFC_ALLOWED_COOKIES=PHPSESSID,sesUID,sesDID,cf_clearance

# Backend Webhook URL (required in production/Docker)
# Used by the webhook client to send sync progress callbacks to the backend.
# Must be set to the backend's Docker hostname when running in containers.
# Example: http://fc-backend:5050
# BACKEND_URL=http://fc-backend:5050

# Aggregation Spine Ingest (optional, default OFF)
# Base URL of the fc-aggregation SpineIngest gRPC server (h2c/HTTP2).
# When set AND a plugin ruleset matches an item's URL, the scrape queue takes
# the new path: raw page fetch -> plugin extraction -> gRPC emit to the spine
# (no webhook leg). Unset = legacy scrapeMFC+webhook path for everything.
# INGEST_BASE_URL=http://fc-aggregation:50051
# Per-call deadline in milliseconds for spine ingest RPCs (default: 30000)
# INGEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Impit Transport Timeout (optional)
# Per-request timeout in milliseconds for the impit browser-TLS transport. Applies
# to EACH impit GET independently -- a session-gated store's homepage prime and the
# target fetch each get the full budget. Raise it for slow session-gated stores
# whose prime/search can take 15-30s. Unset/invalid falls back to the default; any
# value is clamped to [5000, 120000].
# Default: 30000
# IMPIT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# Challenge Cooldown (optional)
# Per-host "leave it alone" window in milliseconds after a store serves a
# Cloudflare challenge/block. While a host is cooling, the scrape queue and the
# lookup fan-out skip it WITHOUT fetching, so repeat challenges stop degrading
# the egress IP's CF reputation. Unset/invalid falls back to the default; any
# finite value is clamped to [60000 (1 min), 86400000 (24 h)].
# Default: 1800000 (30 min)
# CHALLENGE_COOLDOWN_MS=1800000

# Image host policy (optional) — a { host: rule } table saying which BYTES lane, egress,
# referer and user agent each IMAGE host gets. Read from IMAGE_HOST_POLICY_JSON first
# (inline JSON); if that is unset or unusable, from the file named by
# IMAGE_HOST_POLICY_FILE. Neither ⇒ the built-in table, which is the otakumode.com
# permaban alone. The table is read ONCE, at load — an edit needs a restart.
# IMAGE_HOST_POLICY_JSON={"cdn11.bigcommerce.com":{"lane":"http"}}
# IMAGE_HOST_POLICY_FILE=/etc/fc/image-host-policy.json

# Per-Host Pacing (optional, D-11) — two knobs governing the gap between successive
# PRIMARY dispatches to the SAME host in the bulk ingest queue. The interactive
# /lookup fan-out is NOT paced by these. effective =
#   declared != null ? max(declared, HARD_FLOOR) : max(BASE_DELAY, HARD_FLOOR)
#
# SCRAPER_HOST_BASE_DELAY_MS — the DEFAULT gap (ms) for a host that DECLARES NO rate
#   (no resolved store profile). A store that declares its own rateLimit.baseDelayMs
#   is paced by THAT value instead (clamped up to the hard floor), so a clean-API
#   store can run FASTER than this default without slowing every other host. Budget-
#   safe by default: unset/invalid uses 4000ms (24,700 req/day/host = 3500ms at-
#   budget; 4000ms = 12% headroom). LOWER toward 3500 only on clean evidence; a
#   forgotten value must never run an unknown host over budget. Read fresh each call.
# Default: 4000
# SCRAPER_HOST_BASE_DELAY_MS=4000
#
# SCRAPER_HOST_HARD_FLOOR_MS — the ABSOLUTE minimum gap (ms), clamping EVERY host
#   INCLUDING deliberately-declared ones (typo/misconfig protection: a baseDelayMs of
#   40 — or 0 — can never hammer a host, it clamps to this). 1000ms = 1 req/s/host,
#   intentionally BELOW the frontier budget so a declared fast API store can
#   differentiate into the ~1000-3000ms band while a mistake can never exceed 1 req/s.
#   Faster than 1 req/s must be a reviewed exception (lower this). Unset/invalid/<=0
#   uses 1000ms — never 0/no-pacing. Read fresh each call.
# Default: 1000
# SCRAPER_HOST_HARD_FLOOR_MS=1000

# Debug Logging (optional)
# Enable debug output for specific namespaces
# DEBUG=scraper:*
# DEBUG=scraper:mfc
# DEBUG=scraper:browser
# SERVICE_AUTH_TOKEN_DEBUG=false

# Raw HTML Capture (optional, default OFF)
# When true, gzips the raw scraped page HTML and attaches rawHtmlGz (base64),
# htmlSha (sha256 hex), and rawHtmlBytes to the item-complete webhook payload
# for the backend to persist and reprocess. The scraper never writes this to
# any store itself -- it only emits it on the webhook.
# PERSIST_RAW_HTML=true

# Raw Image Capture (optional, default OFF) -- the asset lane of the raw capture sink.
# SEPARATE from PERSIST_RAW_HTML in both directions: images are a different corpus with
# different volume and different rights, so enabling page capture must not start pulling
# binaries, and setting this ALONE (page capture off) is a supported images-only wiring --
# either switch builds the sink, and the lane whose switch is off is refused at the write
# boundary (a counted `disabled` skip), never silently dropped. Both off -> no sink at all.
# Must be exactly "true". Originals are stored unaltered (no gzip) under
# <RAW_STORE_S3_IMAGE_PREFIX>sha256/<aa>/<hex>.<ext>, typed by magic bytes, never shown.
# PERSIST_RAW_IMAGES=true
#
# RAW_STORE_S3_IMAGE_PREFIX -- bucket prefix for the asset lane. Default: raw-img/
# RAW_STORE_S3_IMAGE_PREFIX=raw-img/
#
# RAW_STORE_IMAGE_PUT_TIMEOUT_MS -- the asset lane's own WHOLE-OP budget, separate from
#   RAW_STORE_PUT_TIMEOUT_MS (which stays on the page lanes): an original image is up to
#   10 MiB against a page body's ~30 KB gzipped. Unset/invalid uses the default.
#   Like the page budget it covers the capture's ENTIRE trip to the store (HEAD + PUT
#   under one bound, not one each), and reaching it CANCELS the upload -- an image is
#   re-fetched on the next pass, which is why this lane may have the longer budget.
# Default: 30000
# RAW_STORE_IMAGE_PUT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#
# RAW_STORE_PUT_TIMEOUT_MS -- the page/api lanes' WHOLE-OP budget: how long ONE capture
#   may spend on the store, counting its HEAD, its gzip and its PUT TOGETHER. It used to
#   be read per store call, which quietly made the number an operator set a worst case of
#   twice itself. Reaching it now CANCELS the request at the socket and the body is LOST
#   (a page body is the capture nothing re-fetches), so set it above the store's measured
#   cost rather than near it, and watch `timedOut` on /health/detailed.
# Default: 30000
# RAW_STORE_PUT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#
# RAW_STORE_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES -- hard ceiling on ONE STORED image; a larger body is SKIPPED
#   (counted as tooLarge), never truncated. It does NOT bound what is DOWNLOADED -- the body
#   is already buffered and hashed by the time the sink sees it, so the image fetcher is what
#   must reject on Content-Length and abort the read. Unset/invalid uses the default; clamped
#   to 64 MiB so a typo cannot remove the ceiling.
# Default: 10485760 (10 MiB)
# RAW_STORE_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES=10485760
#
# RAW_STORE_ASSET_QUEUE_SHARE -- the share of BOTH sink queue budgets (RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX
#   depth and RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX_BYTES) the asset lane may occupy; the rest is RESERVED for
#   page bodies. The lanes share one queue but not the stakes: a refused page body is
#   provenance lost for good behind a claim already written, a refused image is one the next
#   crawl pass re-fetches. An asset offered above the share is refused with reason
#   'assetReserve' (so its caller retries) and counted BY THE BUDGET THAT HELD IT --
#   rawStore.stats.assetRefusedReserveDepth / assetRefusedReserveBytes (assetRefusedReserve is
#   their sum) -- and the once-a-minute log line names that budget's knob, RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX
#   or RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX_BYTES.
#   The byte half of the share counts the capture's OWN size (one asset can be a quarter of
#   the whole budget where a slot is only ever a slot); the depth half does not. An empty
#   queue always admits one asset either way -- it displaces nothing.
#   The share measures the QUEUE: captures WAITING for a worker, both lanes combined. A
#   running upload holds no queue slot and its bytes sit OUTSIDE RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX_BYTES, so
#   the sink's footprint is RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX + RAW_STORE_CONCURRENCY captures and
#   RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX_BYTES + RAW_STORE_CONCURRENCY x RAW_STORE_IMAGE_MAX_BYTES bytes: at
#   RAW_STORE_CONCURRENCY=12 as deployed, 500 + 12 captures and 256 MiB + 12 x 10 MiB = 376 MiB,
#   of which the asset lane's share is 375 + 12 captures and 192 MiB + 120 MiB = 312 MiB. An
#   empty queue admits an asset however many images are uploading.
#   Pages go FIRST: a worker takes a waiting page before any waiting asset (FIFO inside each
#   lane), so a page admitted into the reserve waits behind at most one running upload (plus
#   one aged asset when that lane's turn has come -- RAW_STORE_ASSET_MAX_WAIT_MS below). While
#   pages keep coming the assets stay resident, holding their slots and bytes, so a page can
#   be refused queueBytesFull where first-come-first-served would have drained them. The
#   shutdown flush spends RAW_STORE_SHUTDOWN_FLUSH_MS on pages before images -- but it can
#   only reorder what is WAITING: a budget shorter than one PUT's p95 rescues no page when
#   every worker is mid-PUT at SIGTERM (tune the budget against the measured PUT p95).
#   /health/detailed shows the depth by lane (rawStore.stats.queuedPages / queuedAssets), and
#   the hold line quotes it, so a share spent by pages never reads as an image backlog.
#   Set 1 to turn the reservation off. Unset/invalid uses the default; clamped to 1, and a
#   0/negative value falls back to the default -- closing the asset lane is PERSIST_RAW_IMAGES'
#   job, not this knob's.
# Default: 0.75
# RAW_STORE_ASSET_QUEUE_SHARE=0.75
#
# RAW_STORE_ASSET_MAX_WAIT_MS -- how long an asset may wait behind pages before the asset lane's
#   oldest capture goes next. Strict pages-first parked the whole resident asset backlog for
#   the length of a crawler burst (~17 min of every hour): nothing uploaded, every new asset
#   refused assetReserve, and RAW_STORE_QUEUE_MAX named on the hold line although raising it
#   frees nothing -- those captures were short of a worker, not of space. An asset that has
#   waited LONGER than this goes next: ONE of them, then a page again, so out of a page burst
#   the asset lane gets at most every other take and a page never waits behind more than one
#   asset. The window bounds how long the lane waits for its next turn, not how long every
#   asset waits. The shutdown flush is exempt and stays strictly pages first. Unset/invalid
#   uses the default; clamped to 1000 from below (a few milliseconds is round-robin).
# Default: 30000
# RAW_STORE_ASSET_MAX_WAIT_MS=30000

# Publish the residential proxy's scheme://host:port on GET /health/detailed. That endpoint is
# unauthenticated and `configured` already answers the operator's question, so the endpoint's
# address is opt-in. Unset -> only { configured } is reported.
# RESIDENTIAL_EGRESS_HEALTH_DETAIL=true

# Ingestion Initiator (node dist/initiator/run.js -- one bounded discovery pass per CronJob tick)
# The initiator issues ONE GET /lookup per term x store (a single siteId in &stores=), so a slow
# or failing store costs only its own lookup instead of zeroing the pass.
#
# INITIATOR_LOOKUP_RETRY_DELAY_MS -- delay (ms) before the ONE retry a transiently-failed lookup
#   gets: an abort/timeout, a network error, an HTTP 5xx, a 429 or a 408. Any other 4xx is NEVER
#   retried (the same request would be refused the same way). A store gets ONE retry for the whole
#   pass, not one per term. The retry spends the run's request budget like any other call.
#   0 = retry immediately; unset/invalid/negative uses 5000.
# Default: 5000
# INITIATOR_LOOKUP_RETRY_DELAY_MS=5000
#
# INITIATOR_PASS_DEADLINE_MS -- wall-clock ceiling on ONE pass. Past it the pass dispatches
#   nothing further and summarizes what it did, so an over-running pass cannot overlap the next
#   CronJob tick (two passes = two gates = double the egress over the single IP).
#   0 = no deadline; unset/invalid/negative uses 2700000 (45 min).
# Default: 2700000
# INITIATOR_PASS_DEADLINE_MS=2700000
#
# INITIATOR_MAX_REQUESTS -- lookups, retries and ingest POSTs share ONE budget and discovery
#   spends it FIRST, so an under-sized budget silently drops discovered URLs. Size it as
#   stores x terms + stores + stores x maxUrlsPerStore (defaults: 7 + 7 + 35 = 49; default 60).
#
# INITIATOR_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS -- keep ABOVE the engine's LOOKUP_STORE_TIMEOUT_MS (15000 default)
#   plus its assembly time, or the client abort always beats the engine's bounded answer.
# Default: 20000
#
# See the README's "Ingestion Initiator" section for the rest of the INITIATOR_* knobs.
